+# August 28 FISA drafts and advice: a more direct headquarters account, with unresolved edits and routing
+
+The newly acquired court copies of **351 and 350** add Maltbie's own contemporaneous written accounts of an NSLU meeting, draft editing and possible OIPR routing. This advances beyond the September 10 account in 334 that attributes a warrant assessment to him. They still do not supply Bowman's original advice, the draft attachment, an actual OIPR submission or a judicial ruling. Original source bytes, earlier reports and competing accounts remain unchanged.
+
+## Exhibit 351: the reported meeting and further work
+
+[351](https://coop.vaed.uscourts.gov/moussaoui/exhibits/defense/351.pdf), physical page 1, contains Morrow's top-level acknowledgment to Maltbie, displayed **Tuesday, August 28, 2001, 3:56 PM**, and a quoted Maltbie message displayed **08/28/01 01:56PM**. The latter is addressed in its body to Ray and Greg. The court index and rendered fields are retrieval/representation evidence, not native transport logs. No time zone is displayed; no exact elapsed interval or receipt chronology is inferred.
+
+In the quoted Maltbie message, first paragraph, he writes that they have just left a meeting with **Spike Bowman, described as “#1 in NSLU”**. He attributes to Bowman a view that they have even less than he thought and says **“we still don't have a connection to a foreign power”**, even if the identity link described in that paragraph could be made. He describes needing intelligence that the person was actually part of the group or movement rather than merely an individual advising another person. The redacted person's identity is not reconstructed. This is Maltbie's account of the meeting and its reasoning, not Bowman's own contemporaneous note or our ruling on the applicable legal standard.
+
+The next quoted paragraph praises prior work to identify, interview and **“neutralize this guy”**, asks Greg to call about next steps, and says there are still things they can do with LEGAT assistance. The exact phrase and surrounding tasks are preserved. It is not an independently documented executed act or a defined operational instruction; underlying task/action records would test its intended scope. The stated further work is contrary evidence to a blanket claim that every FBI effort had stopped by August 28. It does not prove that those further steps occurred or succeeded.
+
+Morrow's acknowledgment thanks Maltbie for help and continued support. That is a distinct message, but it is not independent corroboration that Bowman's remarks were quoted completely or accurately. NSLU, OIPR, a prosecutor and the court are not interchangeable source labels: this page reports an NSLU meeting and does not document an OIPR referral or court disposition.
+
+## Exhibit 350: six edit responses, and a conditional OIPR stage
+
+[350](https://coop.vaed.uscourts.gov/moussaoui/exhibits/defense/350.pdf), physical page 1, is a Maltbie message to Greg Jones, with Rita Flack copied, displayed **Tuesday, August 28, 2001, 1:29 PM**, subject **“ZM fisa - draft 2”**. The page banner names Flack while the From field names Maltbie; those distinct representation fields are preserved, not treated as competing authors or proof of actual receipt. The body says to see an attachment and responds to the recipient's questions. The one-page PDF exposes no embedded attachment in the passive enumeration. That does not establish whether the original email lacked an attachment or why a native draft is absent from this court representation.
+
+All six numbered items were read. Their source-level content and the dependent tests are retained:
+
+| Item, physical page 1 | Sender's recorded account | Missing input needed to assess effect |
+|---|---|---|
+| 1 | Rita is assembling foreign-power material. **“If we get to the point where we can send this to OIPR”**, it will be added to the LHM. | Actual material, draft attachment, review stages and transmittal; the conditional statement is not an actual referral. |
+| 2 | Verbatim answers to interview questions are said not to be needed; the sender considers the current presentation accurate. | Interview record, questions/comments and both draft texts; neither accuracy nor distortion independently tested. |
+| 3 | The sender criticizes how Harry set out an explanation and favors his own draft's presentation. | Exact earlier wording, context and revision; disagreement about presentation is visible, the effect is not. |
+| 4 | **“I inserted the 'not'”**; wording is added to present a point as the recipient's view. The sender calls the subject a liar and says the joyride material adds nothing to the package. | The specific sentence, negation scope, original comments/interview evidence and draft versions. No claim that a fact was reversed or exculpatory material suppressed is established by this account alone. |
+| 5 | Words are added to cover a point; the remaining material is judged not to add to the package. | The point, original comments and actual additions/omissions. |
+| 6 | A name/spelling supplied by **ALAT-Paris** is used; the paragraph is said to make an **“adequate connection to the group”**. | Underlying Paris information, exact paragraph/version and the criterion being evaluated. No redacted name is inferred from other sources. |
+
+The closing paragraph says the editing aims to tighten language, be more concise and adapt the LHM to a new targeted audience, while saying the earlier LHM is not necessarily wrong. That is the sender's stated rationale, not an independent validation of the edits. Preserve it alongside the explicit content-selection and negation statements; do not adopt either accurate editing or misleading alteration as the resolved explanation.
+
+## What remains in tension
+
+**Group connection versus foreign-power connection.** Item 6 of 350 calls the paragraph's group connection adequate; the quoted Maltbie message in 351 reports that a foreign-power connection is still lacking. Both statements remain visible. They refer to differently worded objects and potentially different review stages, but the exact paragraph, material reviewed and meeting record are missing. We do not declare them equivalent, reconcile them as a settled legal distinction, infer a reversal from the displayed times, or select a controlling account.
+
+**Local urgent request versus conditional routing and negative assessment.** [347](https://coop.vaed.uscourts.gov/moussaoui/exhibits/defense/347.pdf), August 24, physical pages **1–2 and 4/9**, requests emergency authority to search property/effects and a residence. Page 2 attributes alleged group/leader links to the information then held and also preserves uncertainty about plan extent and associates. In 350, foreign-power material is still described as being assembled for a possible OIPR stage; 351 reports the adverse connection assessment. Without the draft and decision trail, these records do not establish which material was included, omitted, sent, reviewed or rejected, or whether OIPR ever received that version.
+
+**The FISA-specific explanation versus the later two-route account.** [334](https://coop.vaed.uscourts.gov/moussaoui/exhibits/defense/334.pdf), physical page 1, quoted Samit update paragraph 1, attributes an insufficient-evidence assessment for both criminal and FISA warrants for the computer to Maltbie, reports INS-related direction and non-pursuit, and strongly disagrees. Exhibit 351 describes a foreign-power/FISA concern; it does not separately give a criminal-warrant analysis. Do not turn that one recorded concern into proof that either criminal authority was unavailable or the wrong standard was applied to it. The specific criminal-review basis and disposition remain a concrete missing-record test.
+
+**Further LEGAT work versus reported non-pursuit and opposition to attendance.** The August 28 further-work statement in 351 and September 10 account in 334 remain side by side. The latter also describes a France-search proposal and RFU opposition to FBI attendance. They could concern different times, acts or changes of position, or reflect inconsistent accounts; those are untested alternatives. Neither blanket abandonment nor completed foreign-search activity is established here.
+
+The earlier [334/347 comparison](../moussaoui-emergency-fisa-comparison-2026-10-28/README.md) remains frozen. Its question about the original headquarters assessment has advanced in a precise respect: 351 now supplies a court copy of Maltbie's own report, and 350 his edit/routing account. Bowman's original advice, complete reviewed package, formal legal referral and actual authorization/search outcomes remain unacquired. These messages concern related actors and requests; they must not be counted as independent corroboration of wrongful obstruction merely because there are more exhibits.
+
+## Reading scope, preservation and feasible continuation
+
+Both new one-page exhibits were read completely at 180 dpi. Five prior pages were reinspected: 334 p. 1 and 347 pp. 1/2/4/9. The prior full nine-page 347 review is reused, not newly repeated. The two new pages returned zero extracted text characters; no OCR, hidden-name reconstruction, native-email authentication, clock alignment or legal adjudication was performed. Four PDF byte/hash/page-count preservation checks passed. These checks establish representation identity, not the truth of every assertion or legal sufficiency.
+
+[SOURCES.json](SOURCES.json) gives URLs, access UTC/local time, byte counts, SHA-256, page counts and selected HTTP response fields. Those present-day transport fields are preserved as metadata; they are not treated as native message dates, global content identities or evidence of historical tampering. [OBSERVATIONS.json](OBSERVATIONS.json) supplies exact physical-page, message/item and report-line locators. [EDIT-AND-ROUTE-COMPARISON.json](EDIT-AND-ROUTE-COMPARISON.json) keeps stated edits, advice, conditional referral, authorization and outcomes separate. [REVIEW-AND-CHECKS.json](REVIEW-AND-CHECKS.json) records the user checklist and actual reading/tests; [TEXT-CHANGES.diff](TEXT-CHANGES.diff) preserves exact note additions, not a purported original-draft diff.
+
+[OPEN-TODOS.json](OPEN-TODOS.json) retains the native draft-2 attachment, earlier versions, recipient questions, interview evidence, foreign-power/Paris material, original NSLU advice, the package and OIPR/prosecutor/court routing/disposition, full message headers and executed alternatives. Each lists what test depends on it, searches completed, the next untried route and a revisit trigger. All 18 original input requirements and five-track acceptance criteria, including FAA correction/administrative dispositions and earlier copy/scope/receipt gaps, remain open. Missing inputs defer dependent tests only.
+
+The court index makes exhibits **349** (August 28 exchange) and **693** (August 24 headquarters-drafted LHM) available next leads. Their bodies have not been acquired or read here. Do not identify either as the missing draft-2 attachment or recover a redacted identity from an index title. The next feasible work is to inspect those actual sources and test explicit version/paragraph joins, not repeat unchanged failed routes or infer a final decision from a title.
+
+Original PDFs, page order, redactions and earlier notes remain unchanged on verified media. The public payload excludes raw scans/rasters/extractions, signatures, contact/identification/account details, private correspondence and strategy. No agency/witness outreach, fees, new request/submission, mailbox access, merge, history rewrite, private push, or conclusion of unlawful obstruction, concealment or conspiracy is part of this review.
